# bluez -- the Bluetooth stack, and specifically bluetoothd.
#
# gnome-bluetooth (and through it gnome-control-center's Bluetooth panel) owns
# no Bluetooth code of its own: it is a client of org.bluez on the system bus.
# So the panel's entire behaviour -- adapters, discovery, pairing, trusting,
# connecting -- is bluetoothd's, and without this package the panel loads and
# shows no adapter. gnome-bluetooth belongs to another chain; this is what goes
# underneath it.
#
# BLFS 12.4, pinned here rather than in versions.env -- versions.env is
# regenerated wholesale from the LFS book by tools/pin-versions.sh.
SRC_URL=https://www.kernel.org/pub/linux/bluetooth/bluez-5.83.tar.xz
SRC_SHA256=108522d909d220581399bfec93daab62035539ceef3dda3e79970785c63bd24c
SRC_DIR=bluez-5.83

# EVERY FLAG BELOW WAS CHOSEN AGAINST A RUN OF configure IN duct/builder, not
# against a reading of configure.ac. bluez has fourteen AC_MSG_ERRORs and its
# AC_ARG_ENABLE defaults split two ways -- `--disable-x` in the help string
# means the feature is ON by default, `--enable-x` means OFF -- so which
# probes actually fire is not something to infer.
#
# --enable-library. OFF by default, and turned on for a consumer that is not
# here yet: pipewire's bluez5 plugin includes <bluetooth/bluetooth.h> and links
# libbluetooth. It is what installs libbluetooth.so, the headers under
# /usr/include/bluetooth, and bluez.pc. Nothing in this chain needs it, which is
# exactly why it would otherwise be left off and rediscovered by the audio
# worker as a missing header.
#
# --enable-hid2hci. OFF by default. It builds the tool that switches HID-proxy
# Bluetooth dongles out of HID mode so an adapter appears at all, plus the udev
# rule that runs it. Without it those adapters are silently absent -- the panel
# is empty and nothing says why. IT IS ALSO WHAT MAKES --with-udevdir MEAN
# ANYTHING: the rule is the only file this package installs under the udev
# directory, and it sits behind `if HID2HCI` rather than `if UDEV`. With
# hid2hci off, --with-udevdir is an inert flag with a comment explaining it,
# which is worse than no flag. Verified by staging the install both ways.
#
# --with-udevdir=/usr/lib/udev. Left to itself configure asks
# `pkg-config --variable=udevdir udev`, and in duct/builder that answers
# /usr/lib/udev today -- so this flag changes nothing NOW and is passed anyway,
# for the reason fuse3's recipe already sets out at length: a package must not
# stage into /lib. duct-filesystem owns /lib as a symlink to usr/lib, tape's
# conflict check exempts directories but a symlink is a file, and eudev's
# udevdir is derived from a --with-rootprefix= that is empty. If that derivation
# ever moves, this package would start shipping /lib/udev/rules.d and take the
# symlink with it. Naming the path removes the lookup.
#
# --disable-systemd. There is no systemd here; elogind provides the login side
# and nothing provides systemdsystemunitdir, which configure.ac line 349 turns
# into a hard AC_MSG_ERROR. THE CONSEQUENCE IS LARGER THAN THE FLAG AND IS THE
# MOST IMPORTANT THING IN THIS FILE: src/org.bluez.service, the D-Bus
# activation file, is installed INSIDE `if SYSTEMD` (Makefile.am lines 52-61),
# so with systemd off it is not installed at all. Nothing will start bluetoothd
# on demand. It has to be started at boot. Confirmed by staging the install and
# listing the tree -- see post-install.sh, which says so on every build.
#   Upstream's file would not have helped either way: it carries
#   Exec=/bin/false with SystemdService=dbus-org.bluez.service, so on a system
#   without systemd D-Bus activation runs /bin/false. Installing it would have
#   produced a bluetoothd that appears activatable and never starts, which is
#   worse than its absence.
#
# --disable-obex. OBEX is ON by default and PKG_CHECK_MODULES(ICAL, libical)
# is a hard error; libical is not packaged. WHAT IS LOST is obexd, and with it
# gnome-bluetooth's "Send Files to Device" and the phonebook/message profiles.
# Pairing, audio and input are unaffected -- they are bluetoothd's, not
# obexd's. Named here so that packaging libical for some other consumer does not
# silently leave this off.
#
# --disable-cups. CUPS is ON by default, but its probe is SOFT: configure asks
# pkg-config for cups_serverbin, gets nothing, and carries on with the CUPS
# conditional still true. That is the "resolves off today for the wrong reason"
# shape this tree keeps finding, and here it resolves only half off. Turned off
# by decision instead. Bluetooth printing is the loss; cups is not packaged.
#
# --disable-client. bluetoothctl needs readline (configure.ac line 333, a hard
# error) and readline is not packaged in this tree -- it is coming from another
# chain, so packaging it here would mean two recipes owning one set of paths,
# which tape treats as a hard install error. TO BE REVISITED THE MOMENT READLINE
# LANDS: bluetoothctl is the only way to pair a device without a working GUI,
# which is exactly the situation someone debugging this package will be in.
# Recorded here rather than in a tracker, in the style of ostree's
# --disable-http2 note.
#
# --disable-manpages. Needs rst2man from python-docutils, which is not
# packaged, and configure.ac line 380 makes it a hard error rather than a
# degradation.
#
# --sysconfdir=/etc so main.conf lands in /etc/bluetooth rather than
# /usr/etc/bluetooth; --localstatedir=/var for /var/lib/bluetooth, which is
# where bluetoothd stores pairing keys.
#
# NOT PASSED, each already off and each named so it stays a decision:
#   --enable-mesh       needs json-c and ell
#   --enable-midi       needs alsa, which is another chain's package
#   --enable-btpclient  needs ell
#   --enable-nfc, --enable-sap, --enable-health, --enable-test, --enable-testing,
#   --enable-experimental, --enable-backtrace (that last one needs elfutils)
# NOT PASSED and ON, deliberately: a2dp, avrcp, network, hid, hog, bap, bass,
# mcp, ccp, vcp, micp, csip, asha, tools, monitor, udev, datafiles. Those are
# the profiles and the daemon's own plumbing; btmon in particular is the only
# packet-level view of what an adapter is doing.
#
# --disable-static IS NOT PASSED, and unusually for this tree that is on
# purpose. bluetoothd links several noinst libtool convenience archives
# (libbluetooth-internal.la, libshared-glib.la), and BLFS does not pass it
# either. Staging the install showed no libbluetooth.a is produced anyway, so
# the flag would buy nothing and risk the convenience libraries.
CONFIGURE_ARGS="--sysconfdir=/etc --localstatedir=/var \
--enable-library --enable-hid2hci \
--disable-systemd --disable-obex --disable-cups --disable-client \
--disable-manpages --with-udevdir=/usr/lib/udev"
